terval x \u2264 -1, f(x)=x +1 is still the minimum. So maybe the critical points where the minimum changes are only at x=-1 and x=1. Let me confirm.\n\nYes, because for x \u2264 -1, even though x\u00b2 -1 and -x +1 cross at x=-2, the minimum is still x +1. So the real changes happen at x=-1 and x=1.\n\nTherefore, f(x) is defined as:\n\n- x +1, for x \u2264 -1\n\n- x\u00b2 -1, for -1 \u2264x \u22641\n\n- -x +1, for x \u22651\n\nThis simplifies the problem. So now, with this piecewise function, I can compute f(a) and f(a+2) in different intervals.\n\nOur goal is to find all real numbers a such that f(a+2) > f(a).\n\nSo let's consider different cases based on the intervals where a and a+2 fall.\n\nSince f(x) is defined differently in three intervals, we need to see where a and a+2 are located. Depending on a, a+2 could be in a different interval. So we need to partition the real line into regions where both a and a+2 fall into specific intervals.\n\nFirst, let's note the critical points for a:\n\nSince f(x) changes at x=-1 and x=1, to find where a and a+2 are located, we need to consider the boundaries shifted by 2.\n\nFor a+2 \u2264 -1 => a \u2264 -3\n\nFor -1 < a+2 \u22641 => -3 <a \u2264 -1\n\nFor a+2 >1 => a > -1\n\nSimilarly, for a:\n\nIf a \u2264 -1, f(a) =a +1\n\nIf -1 <a \u22641, f(a)=a\u00b2 -1\n\nIf a >1, f(a)= -a +1\n\nSo we have different combinations:\n\nCase 1: a \u2264 -3\n\nThen, a+2 \u2264 -1, so f(a+2) = (a+2) +1 = a +3\n\nAnd f(a) =a +1 (since a \u2264 -1)\n\nSo compare f(a+2) =a+3 and f(a)=a+1\n\nSo f(a+2) - f(a) = (a+3) - (a+1) = 2 >0\n\nTherefore, for all a \u2264 -3, f(a+2) > f(a) holds true.\n\nCase 2: -3 <a \u2264 -1\n\nHere, a+2 is in (-1,1] because:\n\nWhen a > -3, a+2 > -1\n\nAnd since a \u2264 -1, a+2 \u22641\n\nTherefore, f(a+2) = (a+2)\u00b2 -1\n\nAnd f(a) is:\n\nIf a \u2264 -1, then f(a)=a +1\n\nSo in this interval, we have:\n\nf(a+2) = (a+2)\u00b2 -1\n\nf(a) =a +1\n\nWe need (a+2)\u00b2 -1 > a +1\n\nLet's solve this inequality:\n\n(a+2)