\\frac{a^2}{3} - b}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the polynomial \\( p(x) \\equiv x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c \\) with real roots \\(\\alpha < \\beta < \\gamma\\), we need to show two inequalities:\n\n1. \\(\\sqrt{a^2 - 3b} < \\gamma - \\alpha\\)\n2. \\(\\gamma - \\alpha \\le 2\\sqrt{\\frac{a^2}{3} - b}\\)\n\n#### Step-by-step solution:\n\n#### Part 1: Show that \\(\\sqrt{a^2 - 3b} < \\gamma - \\alpha\\)\n1. The polynomial can be written in terms of its roots as:\n   \\[\n   p(x) = (x - \\alpha)(x - \\beta)(x - \\gamma)\n   \\]\n\n2. Expanding this product we get:\n   \\[\n   p(x) = x^3 - (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)x^2 + (\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)x - \\alpha\\beta\\gamma\n   \\]\n   By comparing coefficients with the given polynomial, we identify:\n   \\[\n   a = -(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = \\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( a^2 \\) and \\( 3b \\):\n   \\[\n   a^2 = (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3b = 3(\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 3b = (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)^2 - 3(\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n5. Rewrite \\( (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma)^2 = \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + \\gamma^2 + 2(\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n6. Subtracting \\( 3b \\):\n   \\[\n   a^2 - 3b = \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + \\gamma^2 + 2(\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha) - 3(\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 + \\gamma^2 - (\\alpha\\beta + \\beta\\gamma + \\gamma\\alpha)\n   \\]\n\n7. Recognize that \\( (\\gamma - \\alpha)^2 = \\gamma^2 - 2\\gamma\\alpha + \\alpha^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   (\\beta - \\alpha)(\\gamma - \\beta) = \\gamma\\beta - \\beta^2 - \\beta\\alpha + \\alpha\\beta = \\frac{(\\gamma - \\alpha)^2 - (\\gamma - \\beta)^2 - (\\beta - \\alpha)^2}{2} < \\