nes involving x1. \n\nTherefore, in all cases, the first four sums are x1 +x2, x1 +x3, x1 +x4, x1 +x5. Therefore, given that, we can use a2 to find x1 =a2 -x3, since x3 is known from step 4. \n\nSimilarly, the ninth sum a9 is the second largest sum, which should be x3 +x5, since the largest sum is x4 +x5. Then, x5 =a9 -x3. \n\nOnce x5 is known, x4 =a10 -x5. \n\nOnce all variables are known, we can check the remaining sums to ensure consistency. \n\nTherefore, this method should work in general, regardless of duplicates, as long as the sorted sums follow the assumed order. \n\nBut is there a case where this method would fail? Let's try to construct such a case. \n\nSuppose we have numbers x1=1, x2=2, x3=4, x4=5, x5=6. Then pairwise sums are:\n\n1+2=3,\n\n1+4=5,\n\n1+5=6,\n\n1+6=7,\n\n2+4=6,\n\n2+5=7,\n\n2+6=8,\n\n4+5=9,\n\n4+6=10,\n\n5+6=11.\n\nSorted sums: [3,5,6,6,7,7,8,9,10,11]. \n\nTotal sum S=(3+5+6+6+7+7+8+9+10+11)/4=(3+5=8; 8+6=14; 14+6=20; 20+7=27; 27+7=34; 34+8=42; 42+9=51; 51+10=61; 61+11=72)/4=72/4=18.\n\na1=3, a10=11.\n\nx3 =18 - (3 +11)=4. Correct.\n\nx1 =a2 -x3=5 -4=1. Correct.\n\nx2 =a1 -x1=3 -1=2. Correct.\n\nx5 =a9 -x3=10 -4=6. Correct.\n\nx4 =a10 -x5=11 -6=5. Correct.\n\nThen, numbers are 1,2,4,5,6. Check if the sums match. The pairwise sums would be:\n\n1+2=3,\n\n1+4=5,\n\n1+5=6,\n\n1+6=7,\n\n2+4=6,\n\n2+5=7,\n\n2+6=8,\n\n4+5=9,\n\n4+6=10,\n\n5+6=11. Which matches the given sums. \n\nTherefore, even when there are duplicates in the sums (two 5s, two 6s, two 7s), the algorithm works. \n\nAnother test case where x1 +x4 =x2 +x3. Let's set x1=1, x2=2, x3=3, x4=4, x5=5. Then x1 +x4=5, and x2 +x3=5. The sorted sums would include two 5s. \n\nFollowing the algorithm:\n\nS=(3+4+5+5+6+6+7+7+8+9)/4=60/4=15.\n\na1=3, a10=9.\n\nx3=15 - (3+9)=3. Correct.\n\nx1=a2 -x3=4 -3=1. Correct.\n\nx2=3 -1=2. Correct.\n\nx5=a9 -x3=8 -3=5. Correct.\n\nx4=9 -5=4. Correct.\n\nNumbers are 1,2,3,4,5. Checks out. \n\nThus, even when a3 and a4 are both 5 (which is x1 +x4 and x2 +x