ext{(2)}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( x + y \\).\n\n1. First, we add the two equations (1) and (2):\n\n\\[\n(10x + y) + (10y + x) = 75 + 57\n\\]\n\n2. Combine like terms:\n\n\\[\n10x + x + 10y + y = 132\n\\]\n\n3. Simplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n11x + 11y = 132\n\\]\n\n4. Factor out the common factor 11 from the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n11(x + y) = 132\n\\]\n\n5. Divide both sides by 11 to isolate \\( x + y \\):\n\n\\[\nx + y = \\frac{132}{11}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx + y = 12\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x + y \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Among the four functions \\( y=2^x \\), \\( y=\\log_2 x \\), \\( y=x^2 \\), and \\( y=\\cos 2x \\), how many of these functions satisfy the inequality \n\n\\[\nf\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}\\right) > \\frac{f(x_1) + f(x_2)}{2}\n\\]\n\nfor all \\( 0 < x_1 < x_2 < 1 \\)?\n\nA. 0\n\nB. 1\n\nC. 2\n\nD. 3", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many of these four functions satisfy the inequality \\( f\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}\\right) > \\frac{f(x_1) + f(x_2)}{2} \\) for all \\( 0 < x_1 < x_2 < 1 \\). Hmm. Let me start by recalling what this inequality means. It looks like it's comparing the value of the function at the midpoint of \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) with the average of the function values at \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\). So, if this inequality holds, it means the function is concave up (or convex) on that interval, right? Because concave up functions have the property that the midpoint value is greater than the average of the endpoints. Conversely, if the inequality were reversed, it would be concave down (concave). So, the question is essentially asking which of these functions are concave up on the interval (0,1). \n\nAlright, let's list the functions again: \\( y = 2^x \\), \\( y = \\log_2 x \\), \\( y = x^2 \\), and \\( y = \\cos 2x \\). Let's analyze each one by one.\n\nStarting with \\(