ngents are \\( \\frac{1}{t_1} \\) and \\( \\frac{1}{t_2} \\). The angle between the tangents is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\theta = \\left| \\frac{\\frac{1}{t_2} - \\frac{1}{t_1}}{1 + \\frac{1}{t_1 t_2}} \\right| = 1\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic roots properties, we find \\( t_1 + t_2 = \\frac{2k}{p} \\) and \\( t_1 t_2 = \\frac{2h}{p} \\). The angle condition leads to:\n\n\\[\n|t_1 - t_2| = |t_1 t_2 + 1|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions for \\( t_1 - t_2 \\) and \\( t_1 t_2 \\) in terms of h and k, we derive the equation:\n\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt{k^2 - 2ph} = |2h + p|\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides and simplifying, we obtain the locus equation:\n\n\\[\n4(k^2 - 2ph) = (2h + p)^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\left(x + \\frac{3p}{2}\\right)^2 - y^2 = 2p^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the locus of points from which the parabola \\( y^2 - 2px = 0 \\) is seen at an angle of \\( 45^\\circ \\) is the hyperbola:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(x + \\frac{3p}{2}\\right)^2 - y^2 = 2p^2}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider a point $P(u, v)$ from where we will draw tangents to the parabola given by \\( y^2 - 2px = 0 \\). The general equation of a line passing through the point \\( P(u, v) \\) is:\n    $$ y - v = m(x - u) $$\n    \n2. This line will be tangent to the parabola \\( y^2 = 2px \\) if the discriminant of the resultant quadratic equation in \\( y \\) equals zero. Substituting \\( x \\) from the line equation into the parabola equation:\n    \\[\n    y^2 = 2p \\left(\\frac{y - v}{m} + u\\right)\n    \\]\n    Simplifying, we get:\n    \\[\n    my^2 = 2p(y - v + mu)\n    \\]\n    Rearranging and simplifying:\n    \\[\n    my^2 - 2py + 2pv - 2mpu = 0\n    \\]\n    \n3. The discriminant of this quadratic equation in \\( y \\) must be zero for the line to be tangent:\n    \\[\n    (2p)^2 - 4m(2pv - 2mpu) = 0\n    \\]\n    Simplifying the discriminant:\n    \\[\n    4p^2 - 8mpv + 8m^2pu = 0\n    \\]\n    Dividing by 4:\n    \\[\n    p^2 - 2mpv + 2m^2pu = 0\n    \\]\n    Solving for \\( m \\):\n    \\[\n    p = 2mpv - 2m^2pu\n    \\]\n    Rearranging:\n    \\[\n    2u m^2 - 2v m + p = 0\n    \\]\n\n4. Solving the quadratic equation \\( 2u m^2 - 2v m + p = 0 \\) for \\( m \\), we obtain the slopes \\( m_1 \\) and \\( m_2 \\) of the tangents from \\( P(u, v) \\):\n    \\[\n    m_{1,2} = \\frac{2v \\pm \\sqrt{4v^2 - 8up}}{4u} = \\frac{v \\pm \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u}\n    \\]\n    Hence:\n    \\[\n    m_1 = \\frac{v + \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u}, \\quad m_2 = \\frac{v - \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u}\n    \\]\n\n5. To ensure that the two tangents form a $45^\\circ$ angle with each other:\n    \\[\n    \\tan 45^\\circ = 1 = \\frac{m_1 - m_2}{1 + m_1 m_2}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying this condition:\n    \\[\n    1 + m_1 m_2 = m_1 - m_2\n    \\]\n    \n6. Substituting the values of \\( m_1 \\) and \\( m_2 \\) from the previous step:\n    \\[\n    1 + \\left(\\frac{v + \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u} \\cdot \\frac{v - \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u}\\right) = \\frac{v + \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u} - \\frac{v - \\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u}\n    \\]\n    Simplifying inside the parentheses:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{(v^2 - (v^2 - 2pu))}{4u^2} = \\frac{2pu}{4u^2} = \\frac{p}{2u}\n    \\]\n    Therefore:\n    \\[\n    1 + \\frac{p}{2u} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{2u} = \\frac{\\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{u}\n    \\]\n    \n7. Rearranging and squaring both sides to solve for the geometric locus:\n    \\[\n    \\left(1 + \\frac{p}{2u}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{v^2 - 2pu}}{u}\\right)^2\n    \\]\n    Simplifying and squaring:\n    \\[\n    \\left(\\frac{2u + p}{2u}\\right)^2 = \\frac{v^2 - 2pu}{u^2}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    \\left(\\frac{2u + p}{2u}\\right)^2 = \\frac{v^2 - 2pu}{u^2}\n    \\]\n    Let us multiply both sides by \\( u^2 \\):\n    \\[\n    (u + \\frac{p}{2})^2 = v^2 - 2pu\n    \\]\n    Moving everything to one side:\n    \\[\n    (u + \\frac{3p}{2})^2 - v^2 = 2p^2\n    \\]\n    Dividing by \\( 2p^2 \\), we get:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{(u + \\frac{3}{2}p)^2}{2p^2} - \\frac{v^2}{2p^2} = 1\n    \\]\n    This is the equation of a hyperbola. Hence, the geometrical locus is part of the hyperbola centered at \\( (u, v) = \\left(-\\frac{3p}{2}, 0\\right) \\).\n\n    Thus, the equation found characterizes a hyperbola, where the required geometric locus is constrained to the branch further from the parabola.\n\n$\\boxed{\\left(u + \\frac{3p}{2}\\right)^2 - v^2 = 2p^2}$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "From a point \\( P \\) located on the hypotenuse \\( AB \\) of an isosceles right triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), perpendiculars are drawn to the legs. These perpendiculars divide \\( \\triangle ABC \\) into three parts - two triangles and a rectangle. Could the area of each of these parts be less than \\(\\frac{4}{9}\\) of the area of the original triangle?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out this geometry problem. So, we have an isosceles right triangle ABC, with the right angle at C, I assume. The hypotenuse is AB, and there's a point P on AB. From P, we drop perpendiculars to the legs AC and BC. These perpendiculars divide the triangle into three parts: two smaller triangles and a rectangle. The question is asking if each of these three parts can have an area less than 4/9 of the area of the original triangle. Hmm, okay.\n\nFirst, let me visualize this. In an isosceles right triangle, both legs are equal. Let's denote the length of each leg as 1 unit for simplicity. Then the hypotenuse AB would be \u221a2 units long. The area of triangle ABC would be (1*1)/2 = 1/2. Wait, but 4/9 of that area would be (4/9)*(1/2) = 2/9. So the question is, can each of the three parts (two triangles and a rectangle) have an area less than 2/9? Wait, but 4/9 of the original area is (4/9)*(original area). Wait, original area is (1*1)/2 = 1/2. So 4/9 of that is (4/9)*(1/2) = 2/9. So each of the three parts must be less than 2/9. So, the total area of the three parts is 1/2. So if each part is less than 2/9, then