 calculations? Hmm, maybe the answer is general, but the problem seems to ask for specific numerical answers, which makes me think that perhaps the ellipse and hyperbola are specific ones. Wait, maybe the ellipse is (x\u00b2/1) + (y\u00b2/b_e\u00b2) = 1 and hyperbola (x\u00b2/1) - (y\u00b2/b_h\u00b2) = 1, but with some relation between them given by the problem's conditions? But the problem doesn't mention any specific relation other than the points P and Q moving on them with the vector condition.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the ellipse and hyperbola are confocal? That is, they share the same foci. But the problem says F1 is the right focus of the ellipse, and F2 is the right focus of the hyperbola. If they are confocal, then F1 and F2 would be the same point, but the problem refers to them as different foci. So they must not be confocal.\n\nHmm. Maybe we need to keep the foci as variables. Let me proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, part (1) asks to prove that k1 + k2 + k3 + k4 = 0, where k1 is the slope of AP, k2 slope of BP, k3 slope of AQ, k4 slope of BQ.\n\nGiven that P and Q are related by P = \u03bb Q, so coordinates of P are (\u03bb x_q, \u03bb y_q). Since P is on the hyperbola and Q is on the ellipse, we can write their equations:\n\nFor Q on the ellipse: (x_q\u00b2)/1 + (y_q\u00b2)/b_e\u00b2 = 1.\n\nFor P on the hyperbola: (x_p\u00b2)/1 - (y_p\u00b2)/b_h\u00b2 = 1, but x_p = \u03bb x_q, y_p = \u03bb y_q. So substituting into hyperbola equation:\n\n(\u03bb\u00b2 x_q\u00b2) - (\u03bb\u00b2 y_q\u00b2)/b_h\u00b2 = 1 => \u03bb\u00b2 [x_q\u00b2 - (y_q\u00b2)/b_h\u00b2] = 1.\n\nBut from the ellipse equation, x_q\u00b2 = 1 - (y_q\u00b2)/b_e\u00b2. Substitute that into the hyperbola equation:\n\n\u03bb\u00b2 [1 - (y_q\u00b2)/b_e\u00b2 - (y_q\u00b2)/b_h\u00b2] = 1.\n\nSo \u03bb\u00b2 [1 - y_q\u00b2(1/b_e\u00b2 + 1/b_h\u00b2)] = 1.\n\nHmm, this might be a relation that connects y_q with the constants b_e, b_h, and lambda. But perhaps this is not necessary for part (1). Let's