\n- Row-ubiquitous: appears in every row, or\n\n- Column-ubiquitous: appears in every column.\n\nTherefore, for each color, it is either row-ubiquitous or column-ubiquitous. \n\nTo maximize the number of blue cells, we want blue to be row-ubiquitous (appears in every row) and the other 9 colors to be column-ubiquitous (appears in every column). Then, each row must have at least one blue cell, and each column must have at least one cell of each of the other 9 colors. \n\nThis is similar to the part (a) case where blue was row-ubiquitous (equivalent to row-wise) and the other color was column-ubiquitous. \n\nTherefore, the maximum number of blue cells is:\n\nTotal cells - (number of column-ubiquitous colors * number of columns)\n\n= 400 - (9 * 20) = 400 - 180 = 220.\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of blue cells is 220. \n\nBut wait, but if blue is row-ubiquitous, then each row has at least one blue cell. But to maximize blue cells, we can have more than one blue per row. The column-ubiquitous colors need one cell per column, so each column has 9 cells for other colors, leaving 11 cells per column for blue. Therefore, total blue cells would be 11 * 20 = 220. This matches the previous calculation. \n\nHowever, if we make some of the other colors row-ubiquitous, we might be able to have more flexibility. For example, if blue and another color are row-ubiquitous, and the remaining 8 are column-ubiquitous. Then, each row must have at least one blue and one other row-ubiquitous color. Each column must have at least one of each of the 8 column-ubiquitous colors. Therefore, each column has 8 cells for column-ubiquitous colors, leaving 12 cells for blue and the other row-ubiquitous color. \n\nIf we maximize blue in this scenario, each row would have 1 other row-ubiquitous color and 19 blue cells. However, each column has 12 cells available for blue and the other row-ubiquitous color. The total number of cells for the other row-ubiquitous color is 20 (one per row). These 20 ce