 layers:**\n   \n   - **Third layer:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Side length} = 3 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\text{Number of spheres} = 3^2 = 9\n     \\]\n     \n   - **Fifth layer:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Side length} = 5 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\text{Number of spheres} = 5^2 = 25\n     \\]\n\n4. **Sum the number of spheres in the third and fifth layers:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of spheres} = 9 + 25 = 34\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{34}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Three cones with the vertex at point \\( A \\) touch each other externally, where the first two cones are identical, and the third cone has an apex angle of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\). All cones also touch a plane passing through point \\( A \\) and lie on the same side of it. Find the apex angle of the first two cones. (The apex angle of a cone is defined as the angle between its generatrix in an axial section.)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this problem. So, there are three cones with the same vertex at point A. The first two are identical, and the third one has an apex angle of \u03c0/2. All of them touch each other externally and also touch a plane that passes through A. They all lie on the same side of this plane. We need to find the apex angle of the first two cones.\n\nHmm, let's start by visualizing the setup. All three cones share the same vertex A and are touching each other externally. They also all touch a common plane passing through A. Since they lie on the same side of the plane, the plane is tangent to each cone. The apex angle of a cone is the angle between the generatrices in an axial section. So, if we take a cross-section through the axis of the cone, the apex angle is the angle at the vertex between the two lines (generatrices) that form the sides of the cone.\n\nLet me recall that when two cones touch each other externally, their axes are aligned in some way, a