odulo Calculation**: Find positions within the 498-digit cycle:\n   - \\(9997 \\mod 498 = 37\\)\n   - \\(9998 \\mod 498 = 38\\)\n   - \\(9999 \\mod 498 = 39\\)\n4. **Modular Exponentiation**: Compute \\(10^{37} \\mod 499\\), \\(10^{38} \\mod 499\\), and \\(10^{39} \\mod 499\\) (adjusted for modulus 998 using the Chinese Remainder Theorem):\n   - \\(10^{37} \\mod 499 = 42\\) \u2192 \\(42 \\mod 998 = 42\\)\n   - \\(10^{38} \\mod 499 = 420\\) \u2192 \\(420 \\mod 998 = 420\\)\n   - \\(10^{39} \\mod 499 = 208\\) \u2192 \\(208 \\mod 998 = 208\\)\n5. **Determine Digits**:\n   - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{10 \\times 42}{998} \\right\\rfloor = 0 \\)\n   - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{10 \\times 420}{998} \\right\\rfloor = 4 \\)\n   - \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{10 \\times 208}{998} \\right\\rfloor = 2 \\)\n\n**Answer**: \\(\\boxed{042}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given the problem of finding the 3-digit number formed by the $9998^{\\text{th}}$ through $10000^{\\text{th}}$ digits after the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{998}\\).\n\nTo solve this, we need to understand the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{998}\\). First, let's begin with a fundamental observation:\n\n1. **Determine the Periodicity of Decimal Expansion:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{998} \\approx 0.001002004008016032064128256513026052104208416832665330661322644528105210421084216843687374749498997993987975951903807615230460921843687375 \\dots\n   \\]\n\n   From number theory, the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{998}\\) has a repeating pattern because 998 and 10 are coprime. The length of this repeating pattern can be determined by Euler's Totient Function, \\(\\phi(998)\\).\n\n2. **Simplify to Find the Repeating Pattern Length:**\n   \\[\n   \\phi(998) = \\phi(2 \\times 499) = \\phi(2) \\times \\phi(499)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\phi(2) = 1 \\quad \\text{(since 2 is prime)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\phi(499) = 498 \\quad \\text{(since 499 is prime)}\n   \\]\n   Thus, \n   \\[\n   \\phi(998) = 