nFor c=1:\n\nCheck if c=1 is allowed. Wait, earlier we have the recurrence f(x +1) = (1 -c)f(x) +2x +1. If c=1, then 1 -c=0, so the equation becomes f(x +1)=0*f(x) +2x +1=2x +1. So the recurrence would be f(x +1)=2x +1, which is a nonhomogeneous equation without the homogeneous part. But our particular solution when c=1 would be undefined because in the particular solution, a=2/c, which would be 2/1=2, and d=(c -2)/c\u00b2= (1 -2)/1= -1. So f(x)=2x -1, which is one of our linear solutions. Indeed, when c=1, f(x)=2x -1, which works.\n\nSimilarly, for c=-2:\n\nCompute d=(c -2)/c\u00b2= (-2 -2)/4= (-4)/4= -1\n\nThus, f(x)= (2/(-2))x + (-1)= -x -1, which is the other linear solution.\n\nThus, the only solutions that come from the particular solution (k=0) are the two linear solutions.\n\nIf we consider k\u22600, then we have the exponential term. Let's take c=1/2, but wait, c=1 or c=-2 from above. Wait, if we derived that when k=0, c must be 1 or -2, leading to the linear solutions. If k\u22600, then c must satisfy some other constraints.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the only solutions are the two linear functions and the quadratic function.\n\nWait, but earlier we found that the quadratic function f(x)=x\u00b2 -1 also satisfies the equation. How does that fit into this recurrence relation?\n\nIf we take y=1, then for f(x)=x\u00b2 -1, let's check the recurrence:\n\nf(x +1) + f(x)f(1) = f(x) +2x +1\n\nCompute f(x +1)= (x +1)^2 -1 =x\u00b2 +2x +1 -1 =x\u00b2 +2x\n\nf(x)=x\u00b2 -1\n\nf(1)=1\u00b2 -1=0\n\nLeft side: x\u00b2 +2x + (x\u00b2 -1)(0) =x\u00b2 +2x\n\nRight side: (x\u00b2 -1) +2x +1 =x\u00b2 -1 +2x +1 =x\u00b2 +2x\n\nSo equality holds. So for f(x)=x\u00b2 -1, the recurrence holds with c=f(1)=0.\n\nBut in our earlier analysis with the recurrence relation, we had c=f(1). For the quadratic solution, c=0. But earlier, when we derived the recurrence, we started by assuming y=1, and got f(x +1) = (1 -c)f(x) +2x +1\n\nBut when c=0, this becomes f(x +1)=1*f(x) +2x +1 \u2192 f