b\u00b2\n\nFifth term: -g(xy)= -a xy -b\n\nSo combining all terms:\n\n[a(x + y) +b] + [a x\u00b2 y +b x\u00b2 -a y -b] + [a x y\u00b2 +b y\u00b2 -a x -b] + [a\u00b2 xy +a b x +a b y +b\u00b2] -a xy -b=0\n\nNow, let's expand and collect like terms.\n\nStart with expanding each bracket:\n\n1. a x + a y + b\n\n2. a x\u00b2 y +b x\u00b2 -a y -b\n\n3. a x y\u00b2 +b y\u00b2 -a x -b\n\n4. a\u00b2 xy +a b x +a b y +b\u00b2\n\n5. -a xy -b\n\nNow, combine all terms:\n\nTerm by term:\n\n- x\u00b2 y: a x\u00b2 y\n\n- x y\u00b2: a x y\u00b2\n\n- x\u00b2: b x\u00b2\n\n- y\u00b2: b y\u00b2\n\n- xy: a\u00b2 xy -a xy\n\n- x: a x -a x +a b x = a b x\n\n- y: a y -a y +a b y = a b y\n\n- Constants: b -b -b -b +b\u00b2 -b = b - 2b -2b +b\u00b2 -b = b\u00b2 -4b\n\nOther terms:\n\n- x\u00b2 y terms: a x\u00b2 y\n\n- x y\u00b2 terms: a x y\u00b2\n\nNow, collect all terms:\n\n1. x\u00b2 y: a\n\n2. x y\u00b2: a\n\n3. x\u00b2: b\n\n4. y\u00b2: b\n\n5. xy: (a\u00b2 -a)\n\n6. x: a b\n\n7. y: a b\n\n8. Constants: b\u00b2 -4b\n\nFor this expression to be zero for all x, y, each coefficient must be zero.\n\nThus:\n\n1. Coefficient of x\u00b2 y: a =0\n\n2. Coefficient of x y\u00b2: a=0\n\n3. Coefficient of x\u00b2: b=0\n\n4. Coefficient of y\u00b2: b=0\n\n5. Coefficient of xy: a\u00b2 -a=0 \u2192 a(a -1)=0\n\n6. Coefficient of x: a b =0\n\n7. Coefficient of y: a b =0\n\n8. Constants: b\u00b2 -4b =0 \u2192 b(b -4)=0\n\nFrom 1 and 2: a=0\n\nFrom 3 and 4: b=0\n\nFrom 8: 0*(0 -4)=0, which holds.\n\nBut if a=0 and b=0, then g(x)=0, which is the trivial solution. Hence, no non-trivial linear solutions for g.\n\nNext, suppose g is quadratic: g(x)=ax\u00b2 +bx +c. This might lead us back to the quadratic solution. Alternatively, this could get too complex. Given that we've already found three solutions and attempts to find other solutions via different forms haven't yielded anything, it's plausible that these three functions are the only solutions.\n\nAnother approach is to assume that f is differentiable